{"parentUuid":"53d99c19-3a9d-47d8-a925-37205b2ee19e","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"edited_text_file","filename":"C:\\Users\\nsdha\\OneDrive\\binary16\\prime-silo\\runtime\\benny\\synthesis\\engine.py","snippet":"1\t\"\"\"\n2\tSynthesis Engine - LLM-powered triple extraction, embedding, and structural synthesis.\n3\t\n4\tThis module contains the three \"Logic Layers\":\n5\t  A. Relational Graph (NER + Relation Extraction -> triples)\n6\t  B. Conceptual Cluster (Dual-model embedding -> Venn clustering)\n7\t  C. Synthesis Layer (Structural Isomorphism detection)\n8\t\"\"\"\n9\t\n10\timport asyncio\n11\timport json\n12\timport logging\n13\timport os\n14\timport re\n15\tfrom typing import Any, Callable, Dict, List, Optional, Tuple\n16\t\n17\timport httpx\n18\t\n19\tfrom ..core.models import LOCAL_PROVIDERS, call_model\n20\tfrom ..core.schema import IngestionEvent, IngestionEventType, KnowledgeTriple, SynthesisConfig\n21\t\n22\tlogger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n23\t\n24\t# Module-level shared httpx client for connection reuse\n25\t_shared_client: Optional[httpx.AsyncClient] = None\n26\t\n27\t\n28\tdef _get_shared_client() -> httpx.AsyncClient:\n29\t    \"\"\"Get or create a shared httpx client with connection pooling.\"\"\"\n30\t    global _shared_client\n31\t    if _shared_client is None or _shared_client.is_closed:\n32\t        _shared_client = httpx.AsyncClient(\n33\t            timeout=httpx.Timeout(300.0, connect=10.0),\n34\t            limits=httpx.Limits(max_connections=20, max_keepalive_connections=10),\n35\t        )\n36\t    return _shared_client\n37\t\n38\t\n39\t# =============================================================================\n40\t# A. RELATIONAL GRAPH - Triple Extraction via LLM\n41\t# =============================================================================\n42\t\n43\tTRIPLE_EXTRACTION_PROMPT = \"\"\"You are a knowledge graph extraction engine.\n44\tGiven the following text, extract ALL meaningful knowledge triples in the form:\n45\t  (Subject, Predicate, Object)\n46\t\n47\tRules:\n48\t- Subject and Object should be concise noun phrases (concepts, entities, processes).\n49\t- Predicate should be a verb phrase describing the relationship.\n50\t- Extract as many triples as you can find. Be thorough.\n51\t- CROSS-LINKING: If the text describes a design decision, architecture, or pattern, try to link it to potential code symbols or files (e.g., \"AuthenticationService\", \"schema.py\").\n52\t- If the text mentions a source document name, include triples that reference it.\n53\t\n54\tOutput format (STRICT):\n55\t- Respond with ONLY the XML below. Start your reply directly with <triples>.\n56\t- Do NOT use Markdown, bullet/numbered lists, prose, code fences, or <think> tags.\n57\t- Every tag must be well-formed: <object>value</object> (never <object(value)</object>).\n58\t\n59\t<triples>\n60\t  <triple>\n61\t    <subject>Dopamine</subject>\n62\t    <subject_type>Biology</subject_type>\n63\t    <predicate>drives</predicate>\n64\t    <object>reward-seeking behavior</object>\n65\t    <object_type>Concept</object_type>\n66\t    <citation>Dopamine is responsible for...</citation>\n67\t    <confidence>0.95</confidence>\n68\t  </triple>\n69\t</triples>\n70\t\n71\tTEXT:\n72\t{text}\n73\t\n74\tXML TRIPLES:\"\"\"\n75\t\n76\t\n77\tDIRECTED_EXTRACTION_PROMPT = \"\"\"You are an L2 expert reading a specific section of a document.\n78\tYour goal is to extract ALL core points, arguments, or mechanisms made in this section.\n79\t\n80\t{direction_prompt}\n81\t\n82\tRules for Extraction:\n83\t1. Extract as many meaningful triples as you can find. Be thorough.\n84\t2. Filter out any noise; only extract points structurally relevant to the direction (if provided).\n85\t3. \"subject_type\" and \"object_type\" should categorize the entity (e.g., Person, Theory, Technology, Organization, Location, Event, Concept).\n86\t4. \"citation\" must be the exact short sentence/excerpt from the text that justifies the claim.\n87\t5. \"confidence\" should be a score from 0.0 to 1.0 (1.0 = proven/stated as fact, 0.5 = hypothesized).\n88\t\n89\tTEXT SECTION: {section_title}\n90\t{text}\n91\t\n92\tOutput format (STRICT): respond with ONLY the XML block below, starting\n93\tdirectly with <triples>. No Markdown, lists, prose, code fences, or <think>\n94\ttags. Every tag must be well-formed (<object>value</object>). Example:\n95\t<triples>\n96\t  <triple>\n97\t    <subject>Dopamine</subject>\n98\t    <subject_type>Biology</subject_type>\n99\t    <predicate>drives</predicate>\n100\t    <object>Reward-Seeking Behavior</object>\n101\t    <object_type>Concept</object_type>\n102\t    <citation>Dopamine is responsible for the reward-seeking loop in mammalian brains.</citation>\n103\t    <confidence>0.9</confidence>\n104\t  </triple>\n105\t  <triple>\n106\t    <subject>Prefrontal Cortex</subject>\n107\t    <subject_type>Biology</subject_type>\n108\t    <predicate>modulates</predicate>\n109\t    <object>impulse control</object>\n110\t    <object_type>Process</object_type>\n111\t    <citation>The prefrontal cortex acts as a top-down modulator for impulsive actions.</citation>\n112\t    <confidence>0.85</confidence>\n113\t  </triple>\n114\t</triples>\n115\t\n116\tXML:\"\"\"\n117\t\n118\t\n119\tCONFLICT_DETECTION_PROMPT = \"\"\"You are a logical consistency checker.\n120\tGiven the following set of knowledge triples already in the graph, and a NEW set of triples about to be added, identify any CONFLICTS (contradictions) between the existing and new triples.\n121\t\n122\tA conflict exists when:\n123\t- Two triples make opposing claims about the same subject-object pair\n124\t- A new triple directly contradicts an existing one\n125\t\n126\tEXISTING TRIPLES:\n127\t{existing}\n128\t\n129\tNEW TRIPLES:\n130\t{new_triples}\n131\t\n132\tReturn an XML block of conflicts. If no conflicts, return <conflicts></conflicts>.\n133\tEach conflict object should have <concept_a>, <concept_b>, and <description>.\n134\t\n135\tExample:\n136\t<conflicts>\n137\t  <conflict>\n138\t    <concept_a>A</concept_a>\n139\t    <concept_b>B</concept_b>\n140\t    <description>Contradiction explanation...</description>\n141\t  </conflict>\n142\t</conflicts>\n143\t\n144\tXML CONFLICTS:\"\"\"\n145\t\n146\t\n147\tSYNTHESIS_PROMPT = \"\"\"You are a cross-domain synthesis engine that finds structural isomorphisms -\n148\tpatterns that repeat across different fields.\n149\t\n150\tGiven the following set of concepts and their relationships, identify any\n151\tSTRUCTURAL ANALOGIES (isomorphisms) between concepts from different domains.\n152\t\n153\tAn analogy exists when:\n154\t- Two systems from different fields share the same underlying pattern or structure\n155\t- The way one system works is mathematically or structurally similar to another\n156\t- E.g. how a forest fire spreads (Biology) is similar to how a viral tweet spreads (Sociology)\n157\t\n158\tKNOWLEDGE GRAPH:\n159\t{graph_summary}\n160\t\n161\tReturn an XML block of analogies. If no analogies found, return <analogies></analogies>.\n162\tEach analogy should use the tags <concept_a>, <concept_b>, <description>, and <pattern>.\n163\t\n164\tExample:\n165\t<analogies>\n166\t  <analogy>\n167\t    <concept_a>A</concept_a>\n168\t    <concept_b>B</concept_b>\n169\t    <description>Analogy explanation...</description>\n170\t    <pattern>Pattern Name</pattern>\n171\t  </analogy>\n172\t</analogies>\n173\t\n174\tXML ANALOGIES:\"\"\"\n175\t\n176\t\n177\tCROSS_DOMAIN_PROMPT = \"\"\"You are a cross-domain analogy engine.\n178\tGiven the concept \"{concept}\" and its relationships below, explain how this concept\n179\tmaps to the domain of \"{target_domain}\".\n180\t\n181\tCONCEPT RELATIONSHIPS:\n182\t{relationships}\n183\t\n184\tProvide:\n185\t1. The analogous concept in {target_domain}\n186\t2. How the structural pattern maps\n187\t3. Key similarities and differences\n188\t\n189\tBe specific and insightful. Format as XML:\n190\t<analogy>\n191\t  <analogous_concept>...</analogous_concept>\n192\t  <mapping>...</mapping>\n193\t  <similarities>...</similarities>\n194\t  <differences>...</differences>\n195\t</analogy>\n196\t\n197\tXML:\"\"\"\n198\t\n199\t\n200\tCOMMUNITY_NAMING_PROMPT = \"\"\"You are a topological expert.\n201\tGiven the following list of concepts and relationships discovered in a specific \"Semantic Neighborhood\" (community) of a knowledge graph, provide a concise, descriptive name for this community.\n202\t\n203\tCOMMUNITY CONCEPTS:\n204\t{concepts}\n205\t\n206\tGUIDELINES:\n207\t1. The name should be exactly 2-4 words.\n208\t2. It should capture the \"essence\" of the cluster (e.g., \"Neural Execution Layer\", \"User Data Governance\", \"Asynchronous Messaging Patterns\").\n209\t3. Avoid generic names like \"Group 1\" or \"System Cluster\".\n210\t\n211\tReturn ONLY an XML block:\n212\t<community>\n213\t  <community_name>...</community_name>\n\n... [1123 lines truncated] ..."},"type":"attachment","uuid":"76d6d972-2204-4e20-b7cf-6ef32e2ca1fc","timestamp":"2026-06-28T10:45:58.949Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"C:\\Users\\nsdha\\OneDrive\\binary16\\prime-silo\\runtime","sessionId":"b5bb1984-4ef6-4dd6-8984-6e589fd772ec","version":"2.1.187","gitBranch":"HEAD"}
